WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:21.009
Welcome to another episode of Superstars in History, the only talk show that features history's biggest stars.

2
00:00:21.609 --> 00:00:22.650
I'm Faye Moss.

3
00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:26.933
Today's guest is a ruler from the ancient world.

4
00:00:27.633 --> 00:00:34.041
Nearly everybody knows her name, but few people know her secrets.

5
00:00:34.061 --> 00:00:36.827
Maybe I can get her to reveal some of them!

6
00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:43.483
Straight from history, here is the last queen of Egypt, Cleopatra!

7
00:01:01.292 --> 00:01:02.573
Thank you, Faye, dear.

8
00:01:02.853 --> 00:01:04.154
It's lovely to be here.

9
00:01:05.454 --> 00:01:08.696
We're so thrilled to have you, Your Majesty.

10
00:01:09.616 --> 00:01:17.701
You were born in 69 BC, an Egyptian princess in the Ptolemaic dynasty that had ruled Egypt for

11
00:01:17.841 --> 00:01:19.782
275 years.

12
00:01:20.662 --> 00:01:24.604
But your family was actually Greek, not Egyptian, right?

13
00:01:25.605 --> 00:01:30.988
It's true that my ancestors were Greek, but in my heart, I was Egyptian.

14
00:01:31.912 --> 00:01:36.635
I spoke the language—in fact, I was the only ruler in my family who ever did.

15
00:01:37.495 --> 00:01:42.078
I also dressed as an Egyptian and took part in Egyptian rituals and customs.

16
00:01:43.078 --> 00:01:47.561
I felt great pride in my kingdom, even though we were going through hard times.

17
00:01:48.481 --> 00:01:49.462
What was happening?

18
00:01:50.442 --> 00:01:54.164
The people were unhappy, and understandably so.

19
00:01:54.904 --> 00:01:57.866
There were food shortages, and the economy was in trouble.

20
00:01:58.727 --> 00:02:04.518
My father owed a massive debt to Rome, and Rome itself was an ongoing and serious problem.

21
00:02:05.278 --> 00:02:11.322
Rome had been expanding its power throughout the region, and under previous pharaohs, its influence in Egypt had

22
00:02:11.382 --> 00:02:13.603
grown, threatening our independence.

23
00:02:14.644 --> 00:02:18.206
Your relationship with Rome was complicated, wasn't it?

24
00:02:19.087 --> 00:02:26.051
When you were only 18 years old, your father died, and you became queen, ruling alongside your younger

25
00:02:26.131 --> 00:02:27.291
brother Ptolemy XIII.

26
00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.582
Rome helped you solve an early problem.

27
00:02:32.583 --> 00:02:39.048
Yes, my father had left his kingdom to be ruled jointly by us, but Ptolemy, who was only

28
00:02:39.128 --> 00:02:42.991
ten by the way, refused to acknowledge me as co-ruler.

29
00:02:43.591 --> 00:02:47.654
Siblings! I was forced to flee the palace to save my life.

30
00:02:48.755 --> 00:02:55.440
Meanwhile, Julius Caesar, who was then the ruler of Rome, was staying at the royal palace in Alexandria.

31
00:02:56.408 --> 00:03:00.729
I knew that if I could get Caesar's support, I could reclaim my place.

32
00:03:01.649 --> 00:03:05.110
So I figured out a way to sneak into the palace to talk to him.

33
00:03:06.111 --> 00:03:11.392
There's a story that you wrapped yourself in a carpet that was gifted to Caesar.

34
00:03:12.252 --> 00:03:15.213
When the carpet was unrolled, you popped out!

35
00:03:16.493 --> 00:03:18.214
Or maybe it was some type of sack?

36
00:03:19.294 --> 00:03:21.315
They still tell that story?

37
00:03:22.055 --> 00:03:25.536
How I managed to arrange an audience with Caesar is my little secret.

38
00:03:26.516 --> 00:03:30.740
In any case, it worked, and Caesar reinstated me as co-ruler.

39
00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:34.663
This didn't deter Ptolemy from wanting to get rid of me.

40
00:03:35.724 --> 00:03:43.610
Eventually he joined forces with my younger sister, Arsinoe, and attempted to depose me once again, but things

41
00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:45.191
didn't turn out well for him.

42
00:03:46.332 --> 00:03:50.956
He drowned in the Nile after his army was defeated by mine and Caesar's.

43
00:03:51.997 --> 00:03:58.270
From then I had to co-rule with my youngest brother, but at least Caesar secured my position.

44
00:03:59.231 --> 00:04:01.633
It sounds like he helped you a lot.

45
00:04:02.713 --> 00:04:05.876
We helped each other and worked well together.

46
00:04:06.816 --> 00:04:12.501
Rome needed Egyptian grain and depended on our economy to support its expanding territory.

47
00:04:13.362 --> 00:04:17.585
And I persuaded Caesar to return some Egyptian lands that Rome had taken.

48
00:04:18.325 --> 00:04:21.448
This allowed me to reduce taxes and improve our economy.

49
00:04:22.416 --> 00:04:29.743
Caesar also provided Roman legions to protect Alexandria, freeing up my money and troops for other uses.

50
00:04:30.663 --> 00:04:38.750
You may have heard that we had a personal relationship too; we had a son named Caesarion—the heir

51
00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:42.353
to Egypt, and, I had hoped, to Rome as well.

52
00:04:44.075 --> 00:04:47.198
Why don't we talk a little bit more about your reign?

53
00:04:47.938 --> 00:04:49.620
What were your goals as ruler?

54
00:04:51.108 --> 00:04:56.310
More than anything, I wanted to bring back Egypt's glory and independence.

55
00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:02.931
During my reign I restored the kingdom to the size and grandeur it had known a thousand years

56
00:05:02.991 --> 00:05:06.692
before, and Egypt became a world power once again.

57
00:05:07.773 --> 00:05:11.414
But I also wanted to be loved by my subjects.

58
00:05:12.434 --> 00:05:18.896
So I ended taxation on starving farmers and released grain from the royal granaries so my people could

59
00:05:19.056 --> 00:05:24.837
eat. I brought scholars to Alexandria to study and work in our great library.

60
00:05:25.938 --> 00:05:30.579
And I allowed Jews to settle in Egypt and built a synagogue in Alexandria.

61
00:05:31.559 --> 00:05:38.141
As my kingdom was made up of people who spoke many languages, I learned further languages so that

62
00:05:38.221 --> 00:05:40.742
I could communicate with all my people.

63
00:05:41.642 --> 00:05:44.683
The kingdom prospered, and my people worshipped me.

64
00:05:46.263 --> 00:05:47.944
How did Rome react to this?

65
00:05:49.236 --> 00:05:55.902
Relations with Rome were fine under Caesar, but that ended when Caesar was murdered in 44 BC.

66
00:05:57.083 --> 00:06:05.510
Caesar never did name Caesarion as his heir, but I made him my co-ruler after Well, let's just

67
00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:09.033
say that I took steps to get rid of my brother.

68
00:06:11.415 --> 00:06:15.158
You might think it harsh, but it was the way my family did things.

69
00:06:16.539 --> 00:06:21.083
Yikes! I don't want to get on your bad side, so let's move on.

70
00:06:22.825 --> 00:06:25.627
What happened to Rome after Caesar's death?

71
00:06:26.668 --> 00:06:27.649
Rome was divided.

72
00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:33.514
Caesar's nephew, commonly called Octavian, ruled the western half.

73
00:06:34.475 --> 00:06:39.540
Mark Antony, who ruled the eastern half, called me to his side to help him.

74
00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:44.224
Antony was a wise leader and skilled politician.

75
00:06:45.288 --> 00:06:48.350
And you had a relationship with him as well.

76
00:06:49.851 --> 00:06:56.676
We had three children together, but more importantly for this discussion, we became joint rulers of our two

77
00:06:56.836 --> 00:06:58.537
very powerful kingdoms.

78
00:06:59.758 --> 00:07:02.900
Unfortunately Octavian wanted to be rid of us.

79
00:07:03.861 --> 00:07:05.962
He hated—and feared—me!

80
00:07:06.983 --> 00:07:14.368
My son Caesarion had a strong claim to Octavian's throne, and both Antony and I declared him heir.

81
00:07:15.472 --> 00:07:19.313
Octavian did not feel safe as long as we lived.

82
00:07:20.513 --> 00:07:25.275
So Octavian declared war on me and sent his troops after us.

83
00:07:26.355 --> 00:07:28.216
His hatred was personal as well.

84
00:07:29.076 --> 00:07:34.277
Antony was married to Octavian's sister before he left her to support me.

85
00:07:35.317 --> 00:07:36.418
So what did you do?

86
00:07:38.058 --> 00:07:44.380
We fled and ordered ships and soldiers to gather at Actium in Greece to defend our kingdoms.

87
00:07:45.564 --> 00:07:51.869
It wasn't long before Octavian's fleet arrived I can finish the story, Your Majesty.

88
00:07:52.510 --> 00:07:55.292
We're so honored that you joined us today.

89
00:07:55.892 --> 00:07:58.174
We'll let you rejoin history now.

90
00:07:59.115 --> 00:08:00.035
Thank you, Faye.

91
00:08:03.758 --> 00:08:09.723
Cleopatra and Mark Antony's forces were defeated by Octavian at the Battle of Actium in 31 BC.

92
00:08:12.624 --> 00:08:19.070
Antony, after receiving false word that Cleopatra was dead, ended his own life in despair.

93
00:08:20.011 --> 00:08:24.215
Octavian was determined to bring Cleopatra to Rome in chains.

94
00:08:25.195 --> 00:08:33.543
To avoid this fate, Cleopatra took her own life, ending her 22-year reign and briefly leaving Caesarion as

95
00:08:33.723 --> 00:08:43.252
pharaoh. Octavian, now Augustus Caesar, the first Roman emperor, swiftly removed Caesarion and made Egypt part of the

96
00:08:43.352 --> 00:08:44.533
Roman Empire.

97
00:08:45.433 --> 00:08:54.681
Cleopatra's gift for governing and her relationships with two powerful Roman politicians made her an unforgettable pharaoh.

98
00:08:55.602 --> 00:09:01.207
Her story, with its tragic ending, has inspired many retellings over the centuries.

99
00:09:02.388 --> 00:09:04.410
I hope you enjoyed today's show.

100
00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:06.071
See you next time!
